---
/**
 * Privacy, as a template.
 *
 * Micaforge is software other people run. A privacy policy on this site that
 * spoke only for this site would be useless to the person who actually needs
 * one, so this is written as the document an operator adapts, with the
 * placeholders left visible and a warning at the top that nobody can miss.
 *
 * Every factual claim below is checked against docs/CONTRACT.md section 7 and
 * the columns listed in docs/privacy/what-we-store.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import { IconAlert, IconOut } from "../components/react/icons.tsx";

const sections = [
  { id: "who", n: "1", t: "Who this document is for" },
  { id: "collected", n: "2", t: "What is collected" },
  { id: "never", n: "3", t: "What is never collected" },
  { id: "cookies", n: "4", t: "Cookies and local storage" },
  { id: "machines", n: "5", t: "Records of machines" },
  { id: "basis", n: "6", t: "Why it is lawful to collect" },
  { id: "kept", n: "7", t: "How long it is kept" },
  { id: "shared", n: "8", t: "Who else sees it" },
  { id: "rights", n: "9", t: "Your rights, and their limits" },
  { id: "children", n: "10", t: "Children" },
  { id: "changes", n: "11", t: "Changes" },
  { id: "contact", n: "12", t: "Contact" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy · Micaforge"
  description="A plain-language privacy policy template for anyone running Micaforge: what a Micaforge install collects, what it never collects, and the honest limits of a data subject request against data that identifies nobody."
>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main id="main">
    <article class="mf-wrap mf-doc">
      <header class="mf-doc__head">
        <h1 class="mf-doc__title">Privacy policy</h1>
        <p class="mf-doc__lede">
          Written for the person running the software, in language a reader can actually
          check. Micaforge measures traffic rather than individuals, and this document says
          exactly what that does and does not mean.
        </p>
        <div class="mf-doc__meta">
          <span><strong>Template</strong> version 1</span>
          <span>Last changed <strong>22 August 2026</strong></span>
          <span>Covers a Micaforge installation and this project's own site</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="mf-note lg-warn">
        <div class="mf-note__head"><IconAlert size={18} />This is a template, not advice</div>
        <p>
          Micaforge is open-source software that other people install. This document is written
          so an operator can adapt it, and the parts only an operator can fill in are marked{" "}
          <code>[LIKE THIS]</code>. It has not been reviewed by a lawyer, and nothing on this
          page is legal advice.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are running Micaforge: read every section, replace every placeholder, delete
          what does not apply to you, and have somebody qualified check the result before you
          publish it. If you are a visitor reading this on somebody's site and it still contains
          placeholders, the operator has not finished, and you should ask them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <nav class="mf-toc" aria-label="Contents">
        <ol>
          {sections.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.t}</a></li>)}
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <Prose class="lg-body" as="section">
        <h2 id="who">1. Who this document is for</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>[OPERATOR NAME]</strong> (&ldquo;we&rdquo;) runs an analytics installation at{" "}
          <strong>[ANALYTICS DOMAIN]</strong> and uses it to measure traffic to{" "}
          <strong>[YOUR SITES]</strong>. This document describes what that installation records.
        </p>
        <p>
          The software is Micaforge, which is AGPL-3.0 and self-hosted. Nobody but us operates
          it, and it makes no outbound network call while it runs, including for geolocation.
          Your data does not reach the authors of the software, because there is no path by
          which it could.
        </p>

        <h2 id="collected">2. What is collected</h2>
        <p>When you load a page that carries the tracking script, the following is recorded.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>The page.</strong> The address you visited, its title, the query string and
            fragment, and which page you entered and left on.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Where you came from.</strong> The referring site, and any campaign
            parameters in the link you followed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Your device, in categories.</strong> Browser and version, operating system
            and version, device type, screen and window size, language and time zone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Roughly where you are.</strong> Country, region and city, derived from your
            address and then discarded. Map coordinates are the coordinates of the city, not of
            you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>What you did.</strong> Time on the previous page, how far you scrolled, and
            any custom events the site chose to send.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>How fast the page was.</strong> Standard web performance measurements.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A visitor identifier.</strong> Not a name and not a cookie: a one-way hash
            of a daily secret, the site, your address and your browser string. It changes every
            day and cannot be reversed. Tomorrow you are a different visitor to us.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>[If session replay is enabled, say so here.]</strong> Replay records the
          structure of the page and your interactions with it so a visit can be watched back.
          Delete this paragraph if you have not turned it on.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>[If the site calls identify(), say so here.]</strong> A site can attach its own
          account identifier to events for signed-in users. That is a deliberate choice by the
          operator, it is off unless the site's code calls it, and it is the one place where
          data here can be tied to a person.
        </p>

        <h2 id="never">3. What is never collected</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No raw IP address is ever stored on a visitor's record.</strong> It is read
            when the request arrives, turned into a country, a region, a city and a network
            operator, folded into the daily hash, and dropped. There is no column for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No browser string is stored on a visitor's record.</strong> It is read to
            work out the browser, operating system and device type, and those categories are
            what is kept.
          </li>
          <li><strong>No cookie is set and none is read.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>No cross-site tracking, no advertising identifiers, no data sharing.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>No fingerprinting beyond the daily hash described above.</strong></li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="cookies">4. Cookies and local storage</h2>
        <p>
          The tracking script sets no cookies. It writes one value into your browser's local
          storage, <code>mf-opt-out</code>, and only if the site gives you an opt-out control and
          you use it. That value stays in your browser and is never sent anywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          The dashboard we use to read the statistics sets a session cookie for the people who
          log into it. That is us, not you.
        </p>

        <h2 id="machines">5. Records of machines</h2>
        <p>
          Micaforge also records requests from software rather than people: search crawlers, AI
          agents, link previews, monitoring tools. Those records are kept in separate tables and
          carry more detail than a person's record does, including the full client string and a
          salted hash of the source address, because none of it describes a human being.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a request of yours is misclassified as a machine, the effect is that it appears in
          a table of machine traffic rather than in the visitor statistics. It is still not
          linked to you, and no further data is collected about it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="basis">6. Why it is lawful to collect</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>[This section needs your own decision and your lawyer's.]</strong> The usual
          position for cookieless, aggregate measurement in the EU and the UK is legitimate
          interests under Article 6(1)(f) of the GDPR: understanding how a site is used, with no
          profiling, no personal identifiers and no cookie requiring consent under PECR.
        </p>
        <p>
          That position depends on facts about your site and not only on the software. If you
          have enabled replay, if you call <code>identify()</code>, or if your page addresses
          themselves carry personal data, the analysis changes and you may need consent. Decide
          it deliberately and write the answer here.
        </p>

        <h2 id="kept">7. How long it is kept</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>[State your retention period.]</strong> By default Micaforge keeps event
          history indefinitely and session replays for thirty days. An operator can set a
          retention period, after which whole months of history are dropped.
        </p>
        <p>
          The daily secret used to build visitor identifiers is never kept beyond forty-eight
          hours, which is what makes yesterday's identifier permanently unlinkable to today's.
        </p>

        <h2 id="shared">8. Who else sees it</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>[List anybody who does. If nobody does, say so.]</strong> On a standard
          self-hosted install the data sits on our own machine at{" "}
          <strong>[HOSTING PROVIDER, COUNTRY]</strong> and is shared with nobody. There is no
          third-party analytics service behind this, no advertising network, and no data broker.
        </p>
        <p>
          We may publish aggregate statistics, and we may share data where the law requires it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="rights">9. Your rights, and their limits</h2>
        <p>
          Where the GDPR or a similar law applies to you, you have rights of access, correction,
          erasure, restriction, portability and objection.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is a real limit worth stating rather than hiding. Because no cookie is set, no
          address is stored and the visitor identifier changes every day and cannot be reversed,
          we usually <strong>cannot find your data</strong> in order to show it to you or delete
          it. Nothing in the store points at a person. That is a consequence of collecting
          less, not a refusal to help, and under Article 11 of the GDPR we are not required to
          collect more data purely to be able to identify you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where an account identifier has been attached to events, that is different: tell us
          the identifier and we can find and delete those rows. You can also stop collection
          entirely by using the opt-out control at <strong>[LINK, IF YOU OFFER ONE]</strong>, or
          by any browser setting or extension that blocks the script.
        </p>

        <h2 id="children">10. Children</h2>
        <p>
          The site is not directed at children, and nothing collected here identifies anybody of
          any age. <strong>[Adjust if your site is aimed at children.]</strong>
        </p>

        <h2 id="changes">11. Changes</h2>
        <p>
          If this document changes materially we will change the date at the top and, where the
          change affects what is collected, say so on the site.
        </p>

        <h2 id="contact">12. Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Write to <strong>[CONTACT ADDRESS]</strong>.{" "}
          <strong>[If you have appointed a data protection officer or an EU or UK
          representative, name them here.]</strong> You may also complain to your local
          supervisory authority.
        </p>
      </Prose>

      <div class="mf-next">
        <a class="mf-link" href="/docs/privacy/what-we-store">Every column, listed<IconOut size={13} /></a>
        <a class="mf-link" href="/docs/privacy/cookieless-identification">How the daily hash works<IconOut size={13} /></a>
        <a class="mf-link" href="/dpa">Data processing template<IconOut size={13} /></a>
        <a class="mf-link" href="/terms">Terms<IconOut size={13} /></a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lg-warn { margin-top: var(--mf-10); max-width: var(--mf-measure); }
  .lg-body { margin-top: var(--mf-12); }
</style>
